flag is True, and result is To find the average number of badges obtained by a user with over 200 views, you would need to first calculate the count of users who have over 200 views and then divide that count by the count of users who have at least one badge.

Here is the SQL query to achieve this:
```
WITH user_counts AS (
  SELECT COUNT(*) AS user_count, 
    CASE WHEN view_count >= 200 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS has_over_200_views
  FROM users
  GROUP BY user_id
), badge_counts AS (
  SELECT COUNT(*) AS badge_count, 
    CASE WHEN id IS NOT NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS has_badge
  FROM badges
  JOIN postHistory ON badges.id = postHistory.badge_id
  JOIN comments ON postHistory.id = comments.post_id
  JOIN users ON comments.userId = users.id
  GROUP BY badges.id
)
SELECT AVG(badge_count) AS avg_badges
FROM badge_counts
JOIN user_counts ON users.id = badge_counts.user_id
GROUP BY user_counts.user_count;
```
This query first calculates the count of users who have over 200 views by using a subquery with a `CASE` statement to count users who have a `view_count` greater than or equal to 200. It then groups the results by `user_id` and calculates the count of users with over 200 views.

