  INTEGER,\n    source         TEXT,\n    notes          TEXT,\n    link_to_member TEXT\n        constraint income_member_member_id_fk\n            references member\n)\n\nCREATE TABLE "member"\n(\n    member_id     TEXT\n        constraint member_pk\n            primary key,\n    first_name    TEXT,\n    last_name     TEXT,\n    email         TEXT,\n    position      TEXT,\n    t_shirt_size  TEXT,\n    phone         TEXT,\n    zip           INTEGER\n        constraint member_zip_code_zip_code_fk\n            references zip_code,\n    link_to_major TEXT\n        references major\n)\n\n-- External Knowledge: event with highest attendance refers to MAX(COUNT(link_to_event))\n-- Using valid SQLite and understading External Knowledge, answer the following questions for the tables provided above.\n-- What is the event that has the highest attendance of the students from the Student_Club?\nGenerate the SQL after thinking step by step: \nSELECT '}]}
flag is True, and result is To find the event with the highest attendance, we can use the following SQL query:
```
SELECT event.*, MAX(COUNT(link_to_event)) AS max_attendance
FROM event
LEFT JOIN attendance ON attendance.link_to_event = event.event_id
GROUP BY event.event_id
ORDER BY max_attendance DESC
LIMIT 1;
```
This will first join the `attendance` table with the `event` table using the `link_to_event` foreign key. Then, it will group the results by `event_id` and use the `COUNT(link_to_event)` function to count the number of links to each event. Finally, it will sort the results by `max_attendance` in descending order and return the top result by using the `LIMIT 1` clause.

This will give us the event with the highest attendance of the students from the Student\_Club.
