  INTEGER,\n    source         TEXT,\n    notes          TEXT,\n    link_to_member TEXT\n        constraint income_member_member_id_fk\n            references member\n)\n\nCREATE TABLE "member"\n(\n    member_id     TEXT\n        constraint member_pk\n            primary key,\n    first_name    TEXT,\n    last_name     TEXT,\n    email         TEXT,\n    position      TEXT,\n    t_shirt_size  TEXT,\n    phone         TEXT,\n    zip           INTEGER\n        constraint member_zip_code_zip_code_fk\n            references zip_code,\n    link_to_major TEXT\n        references major\n)\n\n-- External Knowledge: DIVDE(SUM(major_name = \'Finance\'), SUM(major_name = \'Physics\'))\n-- Using valid SQLite and understading External Knowledge, answer the following questions for the tables provided above.\n-- What is the ratio between students majored in finance and physics?\nGenerate the SQL after thinking step by step: \nSELECT '}]}
flag is True, and result is SELECT
DIVDE(SUM(CASE WHEN major_name = 'Finance' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END), SUM(CASE WHEN major_name = 'Physics' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END))
AS finance_to_physics_ratio
FROM major;

This query will calculate the ratio between students majored in finance and physics by using the SUM function along with the CASE WHEN operator and the DIVDE function. The DIVDE function calculates the ratio between two values in the form of an integer division.

In this query, the SUM function is used to calculate the total number of students majored in finance and physics. The CASE WHEN operator is used to add a 1 to the count when the major\_name is 'Finance' and 0 otherwise.

The DIVDE function is used to calculate the ratio between the two values obtained using the SUM function, which is the total number of students majored in finance and physics. The result of this query will be a decimal number that represents the ratio between students majored in finance and physics.
