  INTEGER,\n    source         TEXT,\n    notes          TEXT,\n    link_to_member TEXT\n        constraint income_member_member_id_fk\n            references member\n)\n\nCREATE TABLE "member"\n(\n    member_id     TEXT\n        constraint member_pk\n            primary key,\n    first_name    TEXT,\n    last_name     TEXT,\n    email         TEXT,\n    position      TEXT,\n    t_shirt_size  TEXT,\n    phone         TEXT,\n    zip           INTEGER\n        constraint member_zip_code_zip_code_fk\n            references zip_code,\n    link_to_major TEXT\n        references major\n)\n\n-- External Knowledge: top source funds refers to MAX(source) where date_received BETWEEN \'2019-09-01\' and \'2019-09-30\'\n-- Using valid SQLite and understading External Knowledge, answer the following questions for the tables provided above.\n-- Indicate the top source of funds received in September 2019 based on their amount.\nGenerate the SQL after thinking step by step: \nSELECT '}]}
flag is True, and result is SELECT MAX(income.amount) as top\_source\_funds
FROM income
WHERE income.date\_received BETWEEN '2019-09-01' AND '2019-09-30'
GROUP BY income.source;

This query will select the maximum amount of funds received in September 2019 from the "income" table, grouping the results by the source of the funds. The WHERE clause is used to filter the rows based on the date range of September 2019, and the GROUP BY clause is used to group the results by the source of the funds. The MAX() function is used to select the maximum amount of funds from each group, and the results are returned as a single row with the column "top\_source\_funds".
