SELECT e.event\_id, b.budget\_id, b.amount, b.remaining, b.event\_status
FROM attendance a
JOIN member m ON a.link\_to\_member = m.member\_id
JOIN budget b ON a.link\_to\_event = b.event\_id
WHERE b.remaining < 0
) subquery
JOIN (
SELECT event\_id, COUNT(*) as count
FROM attendance
GROUP BY event\_id
) count\_events
ON subquery.event\_id = count\_events.event\_id
/ COUNT\*(SELECT COUNT(*) FROM attendance WHERE b.remaining < 0) \* 100

This query will first select all events that went over budget by joining the attendance table with the budget table and filtering on events where the remaining amount is less than 0. It will then join this result with a subquery that counts the number of attendances for each event, and join that with another subquery that counts the number of events that went over budget. Finally, the query will calculate the percentage of events that went over budget by dividing the number of events that went over budget by the total number of events and multiplying by 100.
